g to force the scope to move back and forth. One of the things I want to caution you with that is that we have found the best repeatability and I know I might get hung out here, but I can only explain to you from experience of I have probably taken more scopes off and re-zeroed a rifle than almost anyone that I know. And probably most of the people in this industry that are selling this stuff simply because we have been running a shooting school for 20 years and every class we take the scopes off to clean the rifles and we put them back on and they always repeat. So I have done this thousands of times, not 10 times, not 50 times, not a chat GBT, or AI, whatever you want to research and development, but I have literally done this hands-on thousands of times. And the best way I found to not just have the best repeatability, especially using something like badger rings and so on and so forth, is to make sure you use torque wrenches, which is something that this gentleman did not do, right? And we'll talk about action screws in the next podcast, but it seemed like they were over-tightened a little bit, but you could also see marks and I'm not sure if that's from him over-tightening it or it trying to move, right? But make sure that in my mind, I don't want that bar that hangs down in the pick rail touching fore or aft. We actually, and I know this sounds counterproductive, but I want you to follow me on this one. We actually find that even with the big magnums, we floated in the middle so that it is not making contact with the pick rail front or back. And there's multiple reasons for this. Number one, when we're clamping down, we have a C-clamp pulling down and drawing that ring onto the actual pick rail. One of the things that I think puts a lot of stress on a scope is the fact that all of these things bend and twist, and we'll talk about yaw and roll here in just a moment as you're shooting. So it's not just recoil coming back, but the actual action and the rings and 