ttle bit. And by the way, we enjoyed having him here at the school. We really liked everything that he did here. It wasn't an issue with the customer to be here. We took a lot of extra time in the class to try to go through his rifle piece by piece and get it working. But the fact that that lubrication, one of the things that we're depending on when we actually put that scope ring down onto the base is we're actually depending on the friction. Once we clamp those two together, those surfaces being able to have a sticky factor to them while the scope is going through recoil as well as roll, yaw, and twist. In which case, now we've made a surface that can actually slip and move around. And we don't want that scope to move 0.0001. We don't want it to move at all. And so I'm just going to share with you my personal experiences with dry mounting and using high quality rings. And, you know, Area 419, of course, says that you can dry mount or wet mount, I believe, on their instructions. And so it's not like this is the only way you can install their scope either. I'm just giving you food for thought because, again, this was being brought up at our school and when I popped the scope off. I was really surprised to see all the lubricant underneath it. So to wrap this up and to talk a little bit more in depth about it, if in my mind, if maximum friction is what our desirable outcome is when we're actually mounting our scope on our rings and think about the anodization being moved to raw aluminum or gripping our scope, I would say the maximum friction should hold true as something that we're desirable or that I would want when we're mounting our rings to our base. So the questions I think that we need to explore is maybe wet mounting gives more consistency of seating during installation. Think of like drawing our scope down and we've got everything lubed up that, you know, nothing can bind. But dry mounting might actually give you more resistance to microscopic movements after 